  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nThe monsoon high is forecast to weaken Sunday into Monday with H5\nheights dropping to 594-596dam, or right around the 90th \npercentile of climatology, by the end of the day Monday. The high \nwill also become flatter and more stretched out across the \nsouthern tier of the CONUS and likely persist through the end of \nthe week. Despite the high weakening, the residual heat dome will \ntake some time to cool. High temperatures are forecast to cool 1-3\ndegrees into Tuesday, from Monday's readings, and another 1-3 \ndegrees into Wednesday. Lows will remain quite warm each day, \nmainly in the mid 80s to around 90F across the lower deserts. The \nextreme heat footprint will decrease as the temperatures decline, \nbut a continuation of the Extreme Heat Warning through Tuesday may\nbe needed for at least the Phoenix area with lingering widespread\nMajor HeatRisk across the metro area based on the current \nforecast. Most other areas will fall to the Moderate risk level \nby Tuesday. Temperatures are then forecast to remain fairly \nsimilar day-to-day Wednesday through the end of the week, with \nminimal synoptic changes, barring any storm influences.\n\nWith the weaker and flatter high, and being on the southern half \nof the high, there will be a longer fetch of easterly winds across\nsouthern New Mexico, southern Arizona, and northern Mexico by \nTuesday. This pattern shift will allow moisture to begin to \nincrease again across southern and eastern Arizona Monday through \nthe middle of the week. As a result, convective potential will \ntick upwards early next week, and the easterly steering flow \nacross southern Arizona will be a better trajectory across the \narea to help spread convection more into the lower deserts by the \nsecond half of next week. Latest NBM/WPC forecast show 10-30% PoPs\nacross south-central Arizona by Wednesday and continuing through \nthe end of the week. Despite lower PoPs on Tuesday, strong outflow\nwinds may at least progress through south-central Arizona. The \nsynoptic pattern next week will open the door for some easterly \nwaves/inverted troughs to progress across the area and enhance \nconvective potential, but it is still too early for models to \nfully resolve any of these features to help determine what day, if\nany, will have better odds for storms across the lower deserts.",
